          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Abraham:</strong> 'Hacking' is the process of compromising and gaining unauthorised access to a computer system or systems, whole networks or digital devices and taking control and inserting malware. This can be motivated by financial gain, protest, information gathering or just for the challenge! Hackers can be from a range of backgrounds and have different motives and may include self-taught 'kids' or rogue IT programmers and professionals skilled at modifying computer hardware and software.</p>
<p>Hacking can involve technical skills which can create “malvertising” or malicious advertising which uses online advertising to spread and install malware. Hackers inject infected ads into legitimate advertising networks that display ads on trusted websites. But hackers also use psychology to trick users into clicking on malicious attachments or providing personal data also known as social engineering.</p>
<p>Hacking is an umbrella term which covers a range of different cyber attacks and has evolved from a lone individual working from their bedroom to a widely established criminal enterprise with turnkey hacking tools available to those with lesser skills.</p>
<p>So, what motivates hackers to commit their crimes.</p>
<p>It may be financial gain such as the theft of credit card details or defrauding individuals and banks. Also holding companies to ransom.</p>
<p>It may be for gaining a reputation in the hacker subculture by pulling off hacks on sites and leaving their 'marks'</p>
<p>It may be for corporate espionage where one company may hack another to steal their information and gain a marketplace advantage or individuals will offer their services for sale.</p>
<p>It may be state sponsored with nations stealing business, military or national intelligence secrets and trying to destabilise other nations.</p>
<p>It may also involve Hacker Activists or Hacktivists who are politically or socially motivated and may wish to raise public awareness and focus public attention on something or someone.</p>
<p>It’s important to remember that an individual or a business may not always be the end target. You may even think why would anyone want to hack me?</p>
<p>But they may be a step along the way. An individual or business may be hacked because of the established connections they have to a higher value target.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
        <section id="hackers"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Hackers</h2>
              <p>The term 'hacker' is often portrayed negatively in the media and purely looked upon as a cybercriminal, however, this is not applicable to all. Hackers are typically divided into 3 types white, black and grey hats. scroll through the slides below to learn about the differences between hackers in more detail.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          White Hat Hackers
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>'White Hat' hackers are also known as ethical hackers and they use their technical skills to protect the world from bad hackers. Government agencies and organisations hire white hat hackers as information security analysts, cybersecurity researchers, security specialists and penetration testers and they often work as independent consultants or freelancers.
A white hat hackers use the same hacking techniques as black hat hackers but they do it with the system owners permission and are motivated to find and fix vulnerabilities before they are exploited and to develop tools that can detect cyberattacks. They work to build security software like antivirus, anti-malware, anti-spyware, honeypots and firewalls and strengthen the overall security of the software and hardware systems.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u1_s3_p2_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Black Hat Hackers
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>'Black Hat' hackers are the true cybercriminals who use their technical skills to defraud and blackmail others. The exploit security vulnerabilities to break into computer systems and networks without permission for financial gain. When we talk about hackers it is the black Hat type we usually think about, sending phishing emails and SMS messages, deploying DDoS attacks, writing, selling and distributing malware, blackmailing victims using ransomware and indulging in a range of corporate espionage, financial fraud and identity theft related crimes. They also ply their trade on the dark web where their services can be accessed by other criminals.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u1_s3_p2_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Grey Hat Hackers
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>'Grey Hat' hackers as the name suggests hackers fall somewhere in between white and black. On the whole their intentions are good but they don't always take the ethical route. They often access systems without permission but not to cause mischief or harm, but to draw owners attention to the existing vulnerabilities of their systems. They will launch the same cyber attacks as white hat hackers to expose loopholes in major organisations, companies and government servers and websites and the reach out to them to inform them of the issues.
They may sometimes charge a fee to fix the problems or strengthen the security system and offer their services to resolve any issues. They don't blackmail the organisations but they will disclose the information publicly if nothing is done to rectify the problems. Grey hats do this to showcase their abilities and gain recognition in the cyber security community, subsequently aiming to advance their careers as security professionals.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u1_s3_p2_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
